\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Positions, orientations, and global constraints}

\p{After a \vocabk{facet move}{frap-000L}, a piece can reach the right
position with the wrong local orientation. The first question is therefore
what data records both the destination and the frame carried by the piece.
\vocabk{Local frames}{frap-000Q} turn this transport into group
coordinates, as in \citet{Section 3.1}{marchetti2025rubik}. We then derive
the [composition law](frap-000R), a frame-independent
\vocabk{total-orientation character}{frap-000S}, and the additional test
that makes this character a conserved quantity for cube face turns.}

\transclude{frap-000Q}
\transclude{frap-000R}
\transclude{frap-000S}
\transclude{frap-000T}
\transclude{frap-000U}
\transclude{frap-000V}

\p{[Transfer](frap-000B) gives a group-theoretic form of the same
frame cancellation and shows how the local group limits what total
orientation can detect.}
